# Who has to look at what — and, just as deliberately, what is left open.
#
# There is no `*` rule here on purpose. A blanket owner would put the founder in
# front of every provider, fixture and doc PR, which is the exact bottleneck R12
# (maintainer burnout, bus factor 1) describes and the exact path a new
# contributor arrives through. Ownership is claimed only where a mistake is
# SILENT rather than loud: the hash and anchor identity core, the security
# boundary, the anchor spec, and everything that decides what gets published.
# Providers, fixtures, recipes, docs, adapters and skills are the contribution
# funnel and are intentionally absent — the harness, the goldens and the
# cross-OS determinism matrix are their review, and those gates do not get tired.
#
# Honest note about enforcement, so this does not become another budget that is
# written down and enforced by nothing (see SECURITY.md on how R9's dependency
# budgets sat unguarded): as of this file's creation `main` carries no branch
# protection and no ruleset, and only one person can merge. So this file is a
# statement of intent, not a control. It becomes a control the day branch
# protection on `main` turns on "Require review from Code Owners" — which is the
# same day the first non-founder maintainer receives a commit bit
# (CONTRIBUTING.md, "Becoming a maintainer"). Turning it on before then would
# only block the sole maintainer from their own repository.
#
# Last matching pattern wins. Order below is grouped by reason, not by path.

# ---- Identity and determinism core -----------------------------------------
# Change one of these and every anchor in every downstream repository can
# re-hash at once. Drift becomes noise, or worse, stops being reported at all.
/src/hash.js            @SherifMoShalaby
/src/jcs.js             @SherifMoShalaby
/src/facts.js           @SherifMoShalaby
/src/anchors.js         @SherifMoShalaby
/src/drift.js           @SherifMoShalaby
/src/resolve.js         @SherifMoShalaby
/src/reanchor.js        @SherifMoShalaby

# The anchor spec is the compatibility contract, and it freezes at 1.0 with a
# written migration policy. Until then, every change to it is a change to
# something other people have committed into their repositories.
/spec/                  @SherifMoShalaby

# ---- Security boundary ------------------------------------------------------
# The redaction barrier (R8: a leaked DSN or key from a DOCS tool), the T2 trust
# machinery that decides whether third-party code runs at all, and the two files
# that determine what a provider can actually read.
/src/redact.js          @SherifMoShalaby
/src/trust.js           @SherifMoShalaby
/src/permissions.js     @SherifMoShalaby
/src/scope.js           @SherifMoShalaby

# The tests that guard those four. A PR that weakens the gate is harder to see
# than a PR that weakens the thing being gated.
/tests/redact.test.js       @SherifMoShalaby
/tests/trust.test.js        @SherifMoShalaby
/tests/permissions.test.js  @SherifMoShalaby

# ---- What gets published, and what proves it -------------------------------
# release.yml holds `id-token: write` and is the only path to npm. action.yml
# and rollup/action.yml run inside other people's CI holding their tokens.
# harness.py carries the R9 supply-chain budget; a change here can turn a gate
# vacuous without turning it red.
/.github/workflows/release.yml  @SherifMoShalaby
/.github/workflows/ci.yml       @SherifMoShalaby
/action.yml                     @SherifMoShalaby
/rollup/action.yml              @SherifMoShalaby
/scripts/harness.py             @SherifMoShalaby
/package.json                   @SherifMoShalaby
/package-lock.json              @SherifMoShalaby

# The extractor runtime lives under providers/ but is not a provider: it is
# eight pinned packages that `pip install` executes on every CI runner that uses
# the Action. Listed after the funnel it sits inside, so it stays owned.
/providers/requirements.txt     @SherifMoShalaby

